# shellcheck shell=bash
use flake

# Route each rustc call through the kache of the host. KACHE_DISABLED=1 runs one
# command without it. CI enters the devshell without direnv, so CI builds
# without kache. The build folder of a worktree sits on the heavy drive when
# one resolves (flake.nix names it), and kache clones a cached crate only
# within one filesystem, so a folder on a drive other than the one that holds
# ~/.cache/kache receives copies rather than clones.
export RUSTC_WRAPPER=kache

# Per-machine answers that are nobody else's: the drive a disk-heavy run should
# absorb, and the lane knobs documented in docs/reference/microvm-verification-harness.md.
# They live outside the checkout, in a file of plain `export` lines. The file
# holds values that the operator chooses. A credential belongs behind a
# credential gateway, not in an environment variable.
project_env=$HOME/.config/project-env/vivarium.env
if [ -r "$project_env" ]; then source_env "$project_env"; fi

# The warnings of the host exobrain CLI. Each one exits 0 and moves nothing.
# The first reads both locks. It warns when the nixpkgs pin of a lock is over
# 30 days old, when a tagged input such as release-kit has a newer release,
# and when the CLI or the hook revisions of .pre-commit-config.yaml are
# behind. The binary ships nix/flake.lock as the first lock of every new
# target, so an old pin reaches every new user, and the developer who runs a
# release sees the warning here. The second warns once a day about
# working-tree copies in the Nix store.
if command -v exobrain >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  exobrain outdated flake.lock nix/flake.lock || true
  exobrain store-guard --daily || true
fi
